libFLAC: a subframe whose residual was left unread is not decoded

read_residual_partitioned_rice_ reads the residual of a subframe partition by partition, and when a partition holds a rice symbol that is not one, it reports lost sync, sets the state to FLAC__STREAM_DECODER_SEARCH_FOR_FRAME_SYNC and returns true, with the rest of the residual never written. read_subframe_fixed_ and read_subframe_lpc_ took the true for a residual and went on to restore the signal from it: every sample after that point is predicted from values nobody wrote, with signed products of them that may overflow, into a frame that read_frame_ then throws away because of the state. MediaPerch's FLAC codec fuzzer, built for Linux with MemorySanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, found a 1676-byte input on which the restore read the unwritten residual that FLAC__memory_alloc_aligned_int32_array had allocated, at -O0 as at -O2. Both return as soon as the state says the frame is lost, as read_frame_ does after each subframe. Against 1.5.0.

diff --git a/src/libFLAC/stream_decoder.c b/src/libFLAC/stream_decoder.c
index 914c9e5c..8c7bc177 100644
--- a/src/libFLAC/stream_decoder.c
+++ b/src/libFLAC/stream_decoder.c
@@ -3132,6 +3132,10 @@ FLAC__bool read_subframe_fixed_(FLAC__StreamDecoder *decoder, uint32_t channel,
 			FLAC__ASSERT(0);
 	}
 
+	/* an invalid rice symbol left the residual unread, and the frame is lost */
+	if(decoder->protected_->state == FLAC__STREAM_DECODER_SEARCH_FOR_FRAME_SYNC)
+		return true;
+
 	/* decode the subframe */
 	if(do_full_decode) {
 		if(bps < 33){
@@ -3235,6 +3239,10 @@ FLAC__bool read_subframe_lpc_(FLAC__StreamDecoder *decoder, uint32_t channel, ui
